# C010 preserved rejected directions (mathematical review, not experiments)

1. Removing K makes arbitrary physical ownership recoverable: false, because
   a consistent finite sample always has the one-block regular witness.
2. Faster DP resolves indistinguishable models: false; different consistent
   ownerships share the exact observed input irrespective of resources.
3. Every dataset is ambiguous: false, P={ab}, N={ba} on ab uniquely forces
   one block. Only nontrivial guaranteed recovery is excluded in this class.
4. Parity same-k complete-language ambiguity already has prime local factors:
   false; pair parity factors decompose. C008's prime finite-horizon deletion
   example has different complete languages and must remain so qualified.
5. No graph edge means independent ownership: false. Doubled pairs P={ε,aabb,
   ccdd,eeff}, N={bbaa,ddcc,ffee} have no old swap/rescue edges but force
   ab,cd,ef. The singleton atom partition is incompatible; fallback is needed.
6. Always replace answers by all atom coarsenings: false when the atom
   partition fails compatibility. Sound edges give only a necessary restriction.
7. A compatible finest atom partition is physical truth: false for r≥2;
   its one-block merge remains observationally possible as well.
8. C009 always enumerated Bell partitions: false; its default already counted
   via DP. Full upfront block-mask preprocessing is a different cost.
9. Lazy unevaluated masks can be assumed zero or omitted: false; compute
   their exact value when needed or prove the branch impossible independently.
10. Remaining-mask bound passing proves feasibility: false. rem=ab, P={ab},
    N={ba} passes full remaining mask coverage, but left=2 has no completion.
11. r=1 requires distinct-atom Bell(r−1) pair output: false; there are no such
    pairs. All existing symbol pairs are same-atom and have count Z=1.
12. Fractions of partitions are physical probabilities: unsupported without
    a specified prior. Exact counts quantify logical alternatives only.

No runs, seeds, logs or timings exist for these documentation-only decisions;
none are reconstructed. No rejected claim is presented as a solver test failure.
